Read-heavy; media-dominated egress.

57
00:03:30.682 --> 00:03:33.157
Scale (assumptions, order of magnitude).

58
00:03:33.437 --> 00:03:34.781
Here is a quick table:

59
00:03:35.061 --> 00:03:36.383
Quantity, and Value.

60
00:03:36.663 --> 00:03:38.306
Messenger monthly users:

61
00:03:38.586 --> 00:03:41.914
2B+ (WhatsApp has reported 2B; VERIFY).

62
00:03:42.194 --> 00:03:48.167
Messages per day: ~100B (reported by WhatsApp in 2020; VERIFY).

63
00:03:48.447 --> 00:03:55.402
Peak message rate: ~2 to 3M messages per second (estimate from daily volume with a 2 times peak).

64
00:03:55.682 --> 00:03:57.005
Concurrent connections:

65
00:03:57.285 --> 00:03:59.887
Hundreds of millions of persistent connections.

66
00:04:00.167 --> 00:04:01.746
Social app daily users:

67
00:04:02.026 --> 00:04:03.498
500M+.

68
00:04:03.778 --> 00:04:06.445
Posts or stories or reels created per day:

69
00:04:06.725 --> 00:04:07.962
Hundreds of millions.

70
00:04:08.242 --> 00:04:09.927
Feed and stories requests:

71
00:04:10.207 --> 00:04:11.743
Tens of billions per day.

72
00:04:13.093 --> 00:04:14.480
Estimation highlights:

73
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:15.677
Connections:

74
00:04:15.957 --> 00:04:31.616
500M concurrent connections at ~1 to 2M per server (achievable with an Erlang or epoll-style design; VERIFY published numbers) means hundreds of connection servers, which are a large, simple, horizontally scaled tier.

75
00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:37.208
Messages: most are small (text, ~100 bytes of ciphertext plus headers).

76
00:04:37.488 --> 00:04:42.373
Media is uploaded once to blob storage, and only an encrypted reference is sent.

77
00:04:43.723 --> 00:04:47.926
Feed: each feed request ranks ~1,000 candidates with a model.

78
00:04:48.206 --> 00:04:53.049
At billions of requests per day, ranking compute dominates the social app's cost.

79
00:04:54.399 --> 00:04:55.273
Part 2.

80
00:04:55.553 --> 00:04:56.513
Requirements.

81
00:04:57.763 --> 00:04:58.638
Messenger.

82
00:04:58.918 --> 00:05:09.457
One-to-one and group chats (up to ~1,000 members; VERIFY current limits), with text, media, documents, voice notes, location, replies and reactions.

83
00:05:09.737 --> 00:05:14.985
End-to-end encryption by default: only participants' devices can read content.

84
00:05:15.265 --> 00:05:21.750
Multi-device: a phone plus up to several linked devices (web, desktop), each with its own keys.

85
00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:29.646
Delivery states: sent (server received), delivered (recipient device received), read (opened), per recipient in groups.

86
00:05:30.996 --> 00:05:37.652
Offline delivery: messages wait on the server (encrypted) until the device connects, with a retention limit.

87
00:05:37.932 --> 00:05:40.535
Voice and video calls, one-to-one and group.

88
00:05:40.815 --> 00:05:47.748
Status (24-hour stories), communities, channels (one-to-many broadcast), business accounts and APIs.

89
00:05:48.028 --> 00:05:50.865
Encrypted backups; account moves to a new phone.

90
00:05:51.145 --> 00:05:55.263
Abuse controls: block, report, forwarding limits, spam detection.

91
00:05:56.613 --> 00:05:57.701
Social feed.

92
00:05:57.981 --> 00:06:03.613
Post photos, carousels and videos with captions, tags and location; edit and delete.

93
00:06:03.893 --> 00:06:06.965
Follow and unfollow; private accounts with approval.

94
00:06:07.245 --> 00:06:11.959
Home feed, stories tray, short-video (reels) feed, explore or discovery.

95
00:06:12.239 --> 00:06:15.119
Likes, comments, shares, saves; notifications.

96
00:06:15.399 --> 00:06:18.258
Search for users, hashtags, places, audio.

97
00:06:18.538 --> 00:06:20.671
Ads in feed, stories and reels.

98
00:06:20.951 --> 00:06:26.050
Moderation: prohibited content removed quickly; appeals; legal takedowns.

99
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.509
Non-functional.

100
00:06:28.789 --> 00:06:30.133
Here is a quick table:

101
00:06:30.413 --> 00:06:31.779
Attribute, and Target.

102
00:06:32.059 --> 00:06:38.800
Message latency: p50 200 ms sender-to-recipient when both are online in the same region.

103
00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:40.040
Durability:

104
00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.632
A message the server has acknowledged is never lost before delivery (within retention).

105
00:06:45.912 --> 00:06:46.701
Ordering:

106
00:06:46.981 --> 00:06:51.461
Per-conversation order preserved per sender; the UI orders consistently.

107
00:06:51.741 --> 00:06:52.659
Privacy:

108
00:06:52.939 --> 00:06:59.787
E2EE content; minimised metadata; no content scanning on the server for E2EE chats.

109
00:07:00.067 --> 00:07:01.155
Feed latency:

110
00:07:01.435 --> 00:07:06.789
Feed first page p95 500 ms; media starts loading immediately.

111
00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:07.944
Freshness:

112
00:07:08.224 --> 00:07:11.573
New post visible to followers within seconds to a minute.

113
00:07:11.853 --> 00:07:19.427
Availability: ≥ 99.99% for messaging; graceful degradation for feed ranking.

114
00:07:20.777 --> 00:07:21.673
Part 3.

115
00:07:21.953 --> 00:07:23.467
High-Level Architecture.

116
00:07:24.717 --> 00:07:26.061
Here is the diagram.

117
00:07:29.911 --> 00:07:30.829
Part 4.

118
00:07:31.109 --> 00:07:32.453
How the Messenger Works.

119
00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:35.196
Registration and keys.

120
00:07:35.476 --> 00:07:42.132
Phone verification: the user registers with a phone number, verified by an SMS or voice OTP.

121
00:07:42.412 --> 00:07:52.396
Key generation: the device generates, on the device: a long-term identity key pair;. a signed prekey (medium-term);. a batch of one-time prekeys.

122
00:07:52.676 --> 00:07:56.281
Publishing: the public halves go to the key directory on the server.

123
00:07:56.561 --> 00:07:58.780
Private keys never leave the device.

124
00:08:00.130 --> 00:08:05.805
Linked devices (web, desktop) generate their own keys and are linked by scanning a QR code.

125
00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:14.234
The phone vouches for them, and contacts' devices learn the new device list (VERIFY WhatsApp's multi-device design in its whitepaper).

126
00:08:15.584 --> 00:08:17.504
Sending the first message to someone.

127
00:08:17.784 --> 00:08:18.744
Fetch keys:

128
00:08:19.024 --> 00:08:27.813
Alice's device fetches Bob's prekey bundle, covering each of Bob's devices: identity key, signed prekey, one one-time prekey.

129
00:08:28.093 --> 00:08:31.613
The server deletes one-time prekeys as they are handed out.

130
00:08:31.893 --> 00:08:33.813
Key agreement (X3DH):

131
00:08:34.093 --> 00:08:39.597
Alice's device computes a shared secret from: her identity key;. a fresh ephemeral key;.

132
00:08:39.877 --> 00:08:43.312
Bob's identity key, signed prekey and one-time prekey.

133
00:08:43.592 --> 00:08:49.011
Bob can compute the same secret later, even though he was offline (§6.1).

134
00:08:50.361 --> 00:08:55.630
Session: a Double Ratchet session is initialised per (Alice device, Bob device) pair.

135
00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:59.558
Each message gets a fresh message key (§6.2).

136
00:08:59.838 --> 00:09:00.691
Encrypt:

137
00:09:00.971 --> 00:09:09.910
Alice's device encrypts the message once per recipient device, including Alice's own other devices so her laptop shows what her phone sent.

138
00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:14.350
It sends the set of ciphertexts to the server over her persistent connection.

139
00:09:14.630 --> 00:09:26.726
Server: the server checks the sender is allowed to message the recipient (not blocked, not rate-limited), then stores each ciphertext in the recipient device's queue and returns sent (one tick).

140
00:09:28.076 --> 00:09:33.217
Delivery: if Bob's device is connected: the connection server forwards immediately.

141
00:09:33.497 --> 00:09:51.439
The device decrypts it, stores it locally and acknowledges; the server deletes it from the queue and tells Alice delivered (two ticks);. if not: a push notification wakes the app (content-free, or with encrypted preview content decrypted on the device), which then connects and drains its queue.

142
00:09:52.789 --> 00:10:00.319
Read: when Bob opens the chat, his device sends a read receipt, itself an encrypted message to Alice's devices.

143
00:10:01.669 --> 00:10:02.757
Group messages.

144
00:10:03.037 --> 00:10:09.736
Naively, sending to a 500-member group means encrypting 500 plus times, once per device.

145
00:10:10.016 --> 00:10:11.829
That is too much work for a phone.

146
00:10:12.109 --> 00:10:30.733
Sender keys (§6.3): each member creates a sender key for the group;. they distribute it to every other member's devices once, over the pairwise sessions;. after that, each group message is encrypted once with the sender key, and the server fans out the single ciphertext to all member devices.

147
00:10:32.083 --> 00:10:39.934
Membership changes: when someone leaves, senders rotate their sender keys, so the removed member can't read future messages.

148
00:10:40.214 --> 00:10:43.691
The group membership service holds member lists and admin roles.

149
00:10:43.971 --> 00:10:47.449
Membership changes are signed events distributed to members.

150
00:10:48.799 --> 00:10:49.631
Media.

151
00:10:49.911 --> 00:11:01.964
Encrypt on the device: the sender encrypts the file with a random key (AES), uploads the ciphertext to blob storage over HTTPS, and receives a URL or ID.

152
00:11:02.244 --> 00:11:08.260
Send the reference: the chat message (E2EE) carries the URL, the media key and a hash.

153
00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:13.255
Download: recipients download the ciphertext, verify the hash and decrypt it.

154
00:11:13.535 --> 00:11:20.895
The server stores only encrypted blobs, which are deleted after all recipients download or after a retention period.

155
00:11:22.245 --> 00:11:31.567
Forwarding: forwarding the same media reuses the uploaded blob where possible, with the same encrypted file and key, which saves storage and bandwidth.

156
00:11:32.917 --> 00:11:34.368
Voice and video calls.

157
00:11:34.648 --> 00:11:41.240
Signalling: call offers go through the messaging channel, encrypted end to end, including the call's media keys.

158
00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:57.072
Media: one-to-one calls go peer-to-peer when NATs allow (ICE with STUN), or via TURN relays;. group calls use relay servers or SFUs;. media is encrypted with SRTP using keys negotiated end to end.

159
00:11:58.422 --> 00:12:05.227
Adaptation: codecs adapt to network conditions, for example, Opus for audio with bandwidth adaptation.

160
00:12:06.577 --> 00:12:07.793
Status (stories).

161
00:12:08.073 --> 00:12:12.511
A status is a message to all contacts with a 24-hour expiry.

162
00:12:12.791 --> 00:12:19.383
It is encrypted like a group message (sender keys per audience), with the audience limited by privacy settings.

163
00:12:19.663 --> 00:12:24.889
Expiry is enforced by clients, and the server deletes the blobs after expiry.

164
00:12:26.239 --> 00:12:27.626
Backups and new phones.

165
00:12:27.906 --> 00:12:32.023
Backups go to the user's cloud storage (Google Drive or iCloud).

166
00:12:32.303 --> 00:12:38.383
End-to-end encrypted backups: the backup is encrypted with a key protected by a user password.

167
00:12:38.663 --> 00:12:46.898
The key is held in a hardware security module-based key vault that limits guessing attempts (WhatsApp's published design; VERIFY).

168
00:12:47.178 --> 00:12:51.829
Moving to a new phone: a device-to-device transfer, or restore from backup.

169
00:12:52.109 --> 00:12:56.546
Contacts see a security code changed notice if identity keys change.

170
00:12:57.896 --> 00:13:00.371
Spam and abuse without reading messages.

171
00:13:00.651 --> 00:13:17.568
The server can't read E2EE content, so it uses: metadata and behaviour: account age, sending rate, how many recipients don't have the sender in contacts, message patterns across many recipients, reports, blocks;. client-side signals: user reports,

172
00:13:17.848 --> 00:13:33.485
which include the last few messages forwarded by the reporting user;. product limits: forwarding limits, for example, forwarded many times labels and limits on forwarding to multiple chats (VERIFY current limits);. enforcement: bans and rate limits.

173
00:13:34.835 --> 00:13:36.691
Classifiers run on metadata.

174
00:13:36.971 --> 00:13:42.902
Research on privacy-preserving techniques continues (VERIFY claims about any specific deployment).

175
00:13:44.252 --> 00:13:45.212
Part 5.

176
00:13:45.492 --> 00:13:47.028
How the Social Feed Works.

177
00:13:48.278 --> 00:13:49.985
Posting a photo or video.

178
00:13:50.265 --> 00:13:59.203
Upload: the app uploads media in resumable chunks to an upload service (Ch 52), with client-side compression for mobile networks.

179
00:13:59.483 --> 00:14:13.371
Processing pipeline: images: resize to several sizes and formats;. video: transcode to an ABR ladder (I06);. extract thumbnails;. compute perceptual hashes (§6.8);.

180
00:14:14.721 --> 00:14:25.132
run classifiers: nudity, violence, spam, and copyright via audio or video fingerprints;. run OCR or captions for accessibility and search.

181
00:14:25.412 --> 00:14:39.876
Create the post: a post object (author, media IDs, caption, tags, location, visibility) is written to the social graph store, with associations: author, then post, post, then tagged users, post, then hashtags.

182
00:14:40.156 --> 00:14:45.063
Moderation outcome: high-confidence violations are blocked before publishing.

183
00:14:45.343 --> 00:14:49.503
Borderline content goes to limited distribution and human review.

184
00:14:50.853 --> 00:14:58.533
Distribution: the post becomes eligible for followers' feeds (§5.3) and, if public, for discovery.

185
00:14:58.813 --> 00:15:01.714
Notifications go to tagged users and close friends.

186
00:15:03.064 --> 00:15:04.152
The social graph.

187
00:15:04.432 --> 00:15:14.864
The model is objects (users, posts, comments, pages) and associations: typed, directed edges with timestamps (follows, likes, authored, tagged, commented).

188
00:15:15.144 --> 00:15:24.083
Queries are overwhelmingly get the association list: the people X follows;. who liked post P;. the latest 50 comments on P;. counts.

189
00:15:25.433 --> 00:15:38.254
Meta's TAO (published 2013; VERIFY) serves exactly this: objects and associations on sharded MySQL, with a large, geographically distributed write-through cache tier.

190
00:15:38.534 --> 00:15:45.169
Reads served from cache; writes going to the leader region's database, and caches updated or invalidated.

191
00:15:45.449 --> 00:15:51.550
Consistency: eventual across regions, with read-your-writes for the author (§6.5).

192
00:15:52.900 --> 00:15:54.244
Building the home feed.

193
00:15:54.524 --> 00:15:56.081
When a user opens the app:

194
00:15:56.361 --> 00:15:57.855
Candidate generation.

195
00:15:58.135 --> 00:16:12.940
Several sources, a few thousand candidates in total: recent posts from accounts followed;. recommended posts from similar-interest accounts (embedding retrieval);. posts from friends' engagement;. ads candidates from the ads system.

196
00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:17.380
Fetching followed content, pull versus push (§6.4):

197
00:16:18.730 --> 00:16:32.618
for most accounts, pull recent posts from each followed account's post list at read time, served from cache;. for users who follow thousands, or for very active authors, precomputed per-user inboxes (push) help.

198
00:16:32.898 --> 00:16:35.053
Most large systems use a hybrid.

199
00:16:35.333 --> 00:16:42.245
Filtering: remove seen, blocked, muted, deleted or policy-violating posts, and apply privacy checks.

200
00:16:42.525 --> 00:16:43.570
Ranking funnel:

201
00:16:44.920 --> 00:17:02.221
a light model scores thousands of candidates;. a heavy model scores the top few hundred, predicting several engagement probabilities (like, comment, share, save, time spent, see less);. they are combined into a value score with weights reflecting product goals (Ch 43).

202
00:17:02.501 --> 00:17:14.448
Final ordering: diversity (not five posts from the same author in a row), freshness, ad slots inserted at spacing rules, integrity demotions (borderline content ranks lower).

203
00:17:15.798 --> 00:17:19.531
Serving: the first page returns post IDs plus metadata.

204
00:17:19.811 --> 00:17:22.137
Media URLs point to the CDN.

205
00:17:22.417 --> 00:17:24.443
The client prefetches the next media.

206
00:17:24.723 --> 00:17:29.523
Pagination uses a cursor, and later pages re-rank with updated context.

207
00:17:30.873 --> 00:17:32.132
Stories and reels.

208
00:17:32.412 --> 00:17:44.615
Stories: the tray order ranks accounts by predicted interest;. each story is short-lived (24 h);. viewers are tracked (author sees who viewed), stored as associations with TTL.

209
00:17:44.895 --> 00:17:53.471
Reels (short video): mostly recommendation-driven, from non-followed creators;. candidates come from embedding retrieval and trending;.

210
00:17:54.821 --> 00:18:06.810
ranking predicts watch time, completion, replays, shares and follows;. strong exploration of new creators;. aggressive prefetching of the next videos, because swiping must feel instant.

211
00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:09.291
Notifications.

212
00:18:09.571 --> 00:18:14.797
Events (likes, comments, follows, mentions, live) generate notification candidates.

213
00:18:15.077 --> 00:18:23.056
A notification ranker decides what to send and when: coalescing (12 people liked your post), frequency caps, quiet hours.

214
00:18:23.336 --> 00:18:29.416
Delivery via push providers (FCM or APNs) and an in-app inbox (Ch 40).

215
00:18:30.766 --> 00:18:32.537
Content moderation pipeline.

216
00:18:32.817 --> 00:18:48.667
Proactive detection at upload: hash matching against known-bad content databases (industry hash-sharing programmes for CSAM and terrorism content; VERIFY programmes);. classifiers for policy categories;. spam and fake-account detection.

217
00:18:48.947 --> 00:18:56.841
Actions: block, remove, restrict reach, age-gate, or send to human review, prioritised by severity and virality.

218
00:18:58.191 --> 00:19:00.196
Human review tools show context.

219
00:19:00.476 --> 00:19:02.247
Reviewer wellbeing measures matter.

220
00:19:02.527 --> 00:19:08.052
User reports and appeals: appeals re-review, with an oversight process where applicable.

221
00:19:08.332 --> 00:19:13.857
Legal: government and court orders processed per jurisdiction, with transparency reporting.

222
00:19:14.137 --> 00:19:22.543
The IT Rules 2021 in India set grievance and takedown timelines for significant intermediaries (VERIFY).

223
00:19:23.893 --> 00:19:24.831
Part 6.

224
00:19:25.111 --> 00:19:26.071
Algorithms.

225
00:19:27.321 --> 00:19:29.092
X3DH key agreement.

226
00:19:29.372 --> 00:19:30.076
Goal:

227
00:19:30.356 --> 00:19:36.820
Alice and Bob agree on a secret while Bob is offline, with mutual authentication and forward secrecy.

228
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:37.761
How:

229
00:19:38.041 --> 00:19:53.401
Alice combines several Diffie, Hellman computations: her identity key with Bob's signed prekey;. her ephemeral key with Bob's identity key;. her ephemeral key with Bob's signed prekey;. optionally, her ephemeral key with Bob's one-time prekey.

230
00:19:53.681 --> 00:19:56.540
The outputs are fed into a key derivation function.

231
00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:03.095
Why it works: only someone holding Bob's private keys can compute the same result.

232
00:20:03.375 --> 00:20:06.597
The ephemeral and one-time keys give forward secrecy.

233
00:20:06.877 --> 00:20:14.365
Verification: users can compare safety numbers (a fingerprint of both identity keys) to detect a man-in-the-middle.

234
00:20:15.715 --> 00:20:16.824
Double Ratchet.

235
00:20:17.104 --> 00:20:23.056
Symmetric ratchet: each message uses a new key derived by a one-way chain (KDF).

236
00:20:23.336 --> 00:20:28.413
Compromising today's key reveals nothing about past messages (forward secrecy).

237
00:20:28.693 --> 00:20:36.651
Diffie, Hellman ratchet: with each reply, the parties exchange new DH public keys and mix the result into the chain.

238
00:20:36.931 --> 00:20:43.693
A compromised device state is healed once new DH values are exchanged (post-compromise security).

239
00:20:43.973 --> 00:20:48.987
Out-of-order delivery: skipped message keys are cached for a bounded number of messages.

240
00:20:50.337 --> 00:20:51.745
Sender keys for groups.

241
00:20:52.025 --> 00:20:57.657
Setup: each sender has a chain key and signing key per group, sent to members over pairwise sessions.

242
00:20:57.937 --> 00:21:02.054
Sending: messages are encrypted once with the chain's current key and signed.

243
00:21:02.334 --> 00:21:04.446
The chain ratchets forward per message.

244
00:21:04.726 --> 00:21:15.883
Trade-offs: efficient fan-out (the server copies one ciphertext);. weaker post-compromise security than pairwise ratchets (rotate on membership change and periodically);.

245
00:21:17.233 --> 00:21:27.729
newer protocols like MLS (Messaging Layer Security, RFC 9420) scale group key agreement more efficiently for large groups.

246
00:21:29.079 --> 00:21:31.127
Feed fan-out: push versus pull.

247
00:21:31.407 --> 00:21:32.751
Here is a quick table:

248
00:21:33.031 --> 00:21:35.783
Strategy, and How, and Good for, and Bad for.

249
00:21:36.063 --> 00:21:37.514
Push (fan-out on write).

250
00:21:37.794 --> 00:21:38.455
How:

251
00:21:38.735 --> 00:21:42.575
On post, insert the post ID into every follower's inbox.

252
00:21:42.855 --> 00:21:43.687
Good for:

253
00:21:43.967 --> 00:21:46.357
Fast reads; users with few follows.

254
00:21:46.637 --> 00:21:47.469
Bad for:

255
00:21:47.749 --> 00:21:50.031
Celebrities with 100M followers:

256
00:21:50.311 --> 00:21:52.125
100M writes per post.

257
00:21:52.405 --> 00:21:53.855
Pull (fan-out on read).

258
00:21:54.135 --> 00:21:54.797
How:

259
00:21:55.077 --> 00:21:58.767
On read, fetch recent posts from each followed account and merge.

260
00:21:59.047 --> 00:21:59.879
Good for:

261
00:22:00.159 --> 00:22:01.631
No write amplification.

262
00:22:01.911 --> 00:22:02.743
Bad for:

263
00:22:03.023 --> 00:22:06.245
Users following thousands: many reads per request.

264
00:22:06.525 --> 00:22:07.335
Hybrid.

265
00:22:07.615 --> 00:22:08.277
How:

266
00:22:08.557 --> 00:22:14.253
Push for most authors; pull for celebrities (above a follower threshold) merged at read time.

267
00:22:14.533 --> 00:22:15.365
Good for:

268
00:22:15.645 --> 00:22:16.349
Both.

269
00:22:16.629 --> 00:22:17.461
Bad for:

270
00:22:17.741 --> 00:22:18.679
Complexity.

271
00:22:20.029 --> 00:22:28.413
Ranking-based feeds reduce the need for complete inboxes: candidates need only be recent enough and plentiful, not exhaustive.

272
00:22:29.763 --> 00:22:31.555
Graph caching and consistency.

273
00:22:31.835 --> 00:22:44.657
Two tiers: write-through caches in front of sharded databases, per region, with leader and follower cache tiers;. writes go to the leader region's database, and invalidations propagate to follower regions.

274
00:22:44.937 --> 00:22:54.003
Read-your-writes for the author: after writing, the author's reads route to the leader tier or carry a version token, so they see their own post immediately.

275
00:22:55.353 --> 00:23:05.956
Hot objects (a celebrity's post with millions of likes) get replicated cache entries and counter sharding (like counts split across sub-counters) (Ch 44).

276
00:23:06.236 --> 00:23:13.745
Lease-based cache fill avoids thundering herds and stale sets, as described in Scaling Memcache at Facebook (VERIFY).

277
00:23:15.095 --> 00:23:16.141
Ranking funnel.

278
00:23:16.421 --> 00:23:22.522
Retrieval: two-tower embeddings (user tower, item tower) plus approximate nearest-neighbour search.

279
00:23:22.802 --> 00:23:25.810
Also graph-based candidates (friends' interactions).

280
00:23:26.090 --> 00:23:32.895
Early-stage ranking: a light model (small neural network or GBDT) over thousands of candidates.

281
00:23:33.175 --> 00:23:38.445
Late-stage ranking: a large multi-task neural network predicting several actions.

282
00:23:38.725 --> 00:23:44.527
Features include user history sequences, author affinity, content embeddings and context.

283
00:23:45.877 --> 00:23:49.355
Value model: a weighted sum of predicted probabilities.

284
00:23:49.635 --> 00:23:55.501
The weights are tuned by experiments on long-term metrics (retention), not only short-term clicks.

285
00:23:55.781 --> 00:24:02.544
Integrity-aware ranking: demote predicted-borderline content, and reduce virality of low-quality content.

286
00:24:03.894 --> 00:24:05.835
ID generation and sharding.

287
00:24:06.115 --> 00:24:06.862
IDs:

288
00:24:07.142 --> 00:24:19.281
64-bit, time-sortable, with shard ID embedded, generated inside the database (Instagram's published approach: timestamp + logical shard ID + sequence; VERIFY).

289
00:24:19.561 --> 00:24:25.641
This keeps IDs unique without a central service and lets the ID route to the shard (Ch 20).

290
00:24:25.921 --> 00:24:34.091
Logical shards: thousands of logical shards mapped onto fewer physical databases, moved as load grows (Ch 11).

291
00:24:35.441 --> 00:24:38.193
Perceptual hashing and near-duplicate detection.

292
00:24:38.473 --> 00:24:51.337
Perceptual hashes (for example, pHash or PDQ; Meta open-sourced PDQ and TMK+PDQF for video, VERIFY) map visually similar images to similar bit strings.

293
00:24:51.617 --> 00:24:52.449
Matching:

294
00:24:52.729 --> 00:24:57.849
Hamming distance below a threshold, looked up with multi-index hashing or LSH.

295
00:24:58.129 --> 00:25:04.828
Uses: blocking known-bad content, detecting reposts, copyright, and spam campaigns reusing images.

296
00:25:06.178 --> 00:25:08.013
Spam and fake-account detection.

297
00:25:08.293 --> 00:25:20.815
Features: registration signals (phone number type, device), behaviour (message or follow rates, reply ratio, block and report rates), network features (connections to known spam clusters).

298
00:25:21.095 --> 00:25:21.885
Models:

299
00:25:22.165 --> 00:25:30.421
GBDT or neural classifiers; graph methods (label propagation, community detection) for coordinated campaigns.

300
00:25:30.701 --> 00:25:33.282
Actions: rate limits, challenges, bans.

301
00:25:33.562 --> 00:25:35.738
Appeals exist for false positives.

302
00:25:37.088 --> 00:25:38.624
Delivery reliability.

303
00:25:38.904 --> 00:25:41.272
Queues: each device's queue is ordered.

304
00:25:41.552 --> 00:25:47.227
A message is removed only after the device acknowledges it, which is at-least-once delivery.

305
00:25:47.507 --> 00:25:54.248
Deduplication: messages carry unique IDs, and clients deduplicate, giving exactly-once display.

306
00:25:54.528 --> 00:25:59.029
Multi-device ordering: the per-conversation order is the sender's sequence.

307
00:25:59.309 --> 00:26:03.235
Clients merge by (server timestamp, sender sequence) for display.

308
