Key transparency: detect a server that could inject keys, with key transparency logs and safety-number verification (VERIFY deployment status).

469
00:32:46.855 --> 00:32:54.237
Account security: two-step verification PIN, device linking approvals, notifications of new devices.

470
00:32:55.587 --> 00:33:03.203
Social data: privacy settings enforced at query time in the graph layer (private accounts, close friends, blocked users).

471
00:33:03.483 --> 00:33:09.307
Deleted content is removed from caches, indexes, backups and training data on schedule.

472
00:33:09.587 --> 00:33:17.864
Children: age assurance, default private accounts for teens, restricted messaging from unknown adults (VERIFY current policies).

473
00:33:18.144 --> 00:33:30.453
India-specific: the IT Rules 2021 obligations for significant social media intermediaries: grievance officers, takedown timelines, monthly compliance reports;.

474
00:33:31.803 --> 00:33:45.243
the traceability (first originator) requirement for messaging, which conflicts with E2EE and is under legal challenge (VERIFY current status);. the DPDP Act 2023 (VERIFY).

475
00:33:46.593 --> 00:33:47.447
Part 10.

476
00:33:47.727 --> 00:33:49.647
Reliability and Operations.

477
00:33:50.897 --> 00:34:02.673
Connection tier: stateless beyond sockets, so clients reconnect to any server;. routing registry updates on reconnect;. rolling deploys drain connections gradually, not all at once.

478
00:34:02.953 --> 00:34:04.787
Offline queues are replicated.

479
00:34:05.067 --> 00:34:09.249
A queue shard failure fails over without losing acknowledged messages.

480
00:34:09.529 --> 00:34:12.963
Regional failover: users connect to the nearest region;.

481
00:34:14.313 --> 00:34:23.337
messages route across regions to the recipient's connection;. the social graph has a leader region per shard, with follower regions serving reads.

482
00:34:23.617 --> 00:34:35.543
Degradation: feed falls back to a simpler ranking (chronological with light scoring) if ranking services fail;. stories still load;. messaging stays independent of social features.

483
00:34:36.893 --> 00:34:37.682
Events:

484
00:34:37.962 --> 00:34:42.933
New Year's Eve, and festivals like Diwali, bring messaging peaks (greetings, media).

485
00:34:43.213 --> 00:34:47.287
Prepare capacity for media upload and delivery, and forwarding spikes.

486
00:34:48.637 --> 00:34:49.640
Part 11.

487
00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:50.965
Key Trade-offs.

488
00:34:52.215 --> 00:34:53.559
Here is a quick table:

489
00:34:53.839 --> 00:34:56.357
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

490
00:34:56.637 --> 00:34:57.810
Message storage.

491
00:34:58.090 --> 00:34:58.922
Chosen:

492
00:34:59.202 --> 00:35:01.527
Device-side history; server relays.

493
00:35:01.807 --> 00:35:02.981
Accepted cost:

494
00:35:03.261 --> 00:35:06.375
Harder multi-device history sync; backups needed.

495
00:35:06.655 --> 00:35:07.722
Group encryption.

496
00:35:08.002 --> 00:35:08.834
Chosen:

497
00:35:09.114 --> 00:35:11.909
Sender keys (MLS for large groups later).

498
00:35:12.189 --> 00:35:13.362
Accepted cost:

499
00:35:13.642 --> 00:35:16.437
Weaker post-compromise security than pairwise.

500
00:35:16.717 --> 00:35:18.679
Abuse control in E2EE.

501
00:35:18.959 --> 00:35:19.791
Chosen:

502
00:35:20.071 --> 00:35:22.397
Metadata, reports, product limits.

503
00:35:22.677 --> 00:35:23.850
Accepted cost:

504
00:35:24.130 --> 00:35:26.242
Less visibility than content scanning.

505
00:35:26.522 --> 00:35:27.610
Feed fan-out.

506
00:35:27.890 --> 00:35:28.722
Chosen:

507
00:35:29.002 --> 00:35:30.367
Hybrid push or pull.

508
00:35:30.647 --> 00:35:31.821
Accepted cost:

509
00:35:32.101 --> 00:35:33.210
Two code paths.

510
00:35:33.490 --> 00:35:34.727
Graph consistency.

511
00:35:35.007 --> 00:35:35.839
Chosen:

512
00:35:36.119 --> 00:35:39.277
Eventual across regions, read-your-writes for authors.

513
00:35:39.557 --> 00:35:40.730
Accepted cost:

514
00:35:41.010 --> 00:35:43.037
Brief inconsistency for others.

515
00:35:44.387 --> 00:35:45.219
Ranking.

516
00:35:45.499 --> 00:35:46.331
Chosen:

517
00:35:46.611 --> 00:35:49.256
Multi-stage funnel with long-term metrics.

518
00:35:49.536 --> 00:35:50.709
Accepted cost:

519
00:35:50.989 --> 00:35:53.571
Heavy compute; complex experimentation.

520
00:35:53.851 --> 00:35:54.853
Moderation.

521
00:35:55.133 --> 00:35:55.965
Chosen:

522
00:35:56.245 --> 00:35:58.613
Proactive detection + human review.

523
00:35:58.893 --> 00:36:00.067
Accepted cost:

524
00:36:00.347 --> 00:36:02.523
Cost; false positives need appeals.

525
00:36:03.873 --> 00:36:04.811
Part 12.

526
00:36:05.091 --> 00:36:06.435
Interview Q and A.

527
00:36:07.685 --> 00:36:08.517
Beginner.

528
00:36:08.797 --> 00:36:09.821
Question 1.

529
00:36:10.101 --> 00:36:12.725
How does a message get from one phone to another?

530
00:36:13.005 --> 00:36:14.371
Thirty-second answer:

531
00:36:14.651 --> 00:36:35.451
The sender's app encrypts it for each recipient device and sends it over a persistent connection; the server stores it in the recipient's queue and acknowledges (sent); if the recipient is online the server forwards it, otherwise a push notification wakes the app to fetch it; the recipient acknowledges (delivered) and later sends a read receipt.

532
00:36:36.801 --> 00:36:37.803
Question 2.

533
00:36:38.083 --> 00:36:40.643
What does end-to-end encryption mean for the server?

534
00:36:40.923 --> 00:36:42.289
Thirty-second answer:

535
00:36:42.569 --> 00:36:52.894
The server only relays and stores ciphertext; keys live on devices, so the server can't read messages, but it still sees metadata like who talks to whom and when.

536
00:36:54.244 --> 00:36:55.289
Question 3.

537
00:36:55.569 --> 00:36:57.425
What happens when you post a photo?

538
00:36:57.705 --> 00:36:59.071
Thirty-second answer:

539
00:36:59.351 --> 00:37:14.775
Resumable upload, processing (resizes, formats, hashes, classifiers), a post object and associations in the social graph, moderation outcome, then the post becomes a candidate for followers' feeds and triggers notifications.

540
00:37:16.125 --> 00:37:17.191
Question 4.

541
00:37:17.471 --> 00:37:19.263
What are the ticks in a messaging app?

542
00:37:19.543 --> 00:37:20.909
Thirty-second answer:

543
00:37:21.189 --> 00:37:23.685
One tick: the server accepted the message.

544
00:37:23.965 --> 00:37:27.186
Two ticks: a recipient device received and decrypted it.

545
00:37:27.466 --> 00:37:33.994
Blue or read: the recipient opened the chat and sent a read receipt (if enabled), in groups, per recipient.

546
00:37:35.344 --> 00:37:36.347
Intermediate.

547
00:37:36.627 --> 00:37:37.736
Question 5.

548
00:37:38.016 --> 00:37:41.749
How do group messages stay efficient with end-to-end encryption?

549
00:37:42.029 --> 00:37:43.395
Thirty-second answer:

550
00:37:43.675 --> 00:38:00.656
Sender keys: each member distributes a group sender key once via pairwise encrypted sessions; each message is then encrypted once and the server fans out a single ciphertext; keys rotate when membership changes; MLS improves this for very large groups.

551
00:38:02.006 --> 00:38:03.115
Question 6.

552
00:38:03.395 --> 00:38:05.230
Push or pull for building feeds?

553
00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:06.875
Thirty-second answer:

554
00:38:07.155 --> 00:38:27.742
Push writes post IDs to followers' inboxes (fast reads, heavy writes for celebrities); pull merges followed accounts' recent posts at read time (no write amplification, heavy reads for users following many); production systems use a hybrid with a follower threshold, and ranking reduces the need for complete inboxes.

555
00:38:29.092 --> 00:38:30.180
Question 7.

556
00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:32.124
How do you store the social graph?

557
00:38:32.404 --> 00:38:33.769
Thirty-second answer:

558
00:38:34.049 --> 00:38:50.412
Objects and typed associations in sharded relational databases fronted by a large write-through cache tier per region (TAO-style); most queries fetch association lists and counts; writes go to the leader region and invalidate caches elsewhere.

559
00:38:51.762 --> 00:38:52.786
Question 8.

560
00:38:53.066 --> 00:38:55.946
How do media files work in an encrypted messenger?

561
00:38:56.226 --> 00:38:57.591
Thirty-second answer:

562
00:38:57.871 --> 00:39:16.069
The sender encrypts the file with a random key, uploads the ciphertext to blob storage, and sends the URL, key and hash inside the E2EE message; recipients download, verify and decrypt; the server keeps only ciphertext and deletes after delivery or retention.

563
00:39:17.419 --> 00:39:18.485
Question 9.

564
00:39:18.765 --> 00:39:21.389
How do notifications avoid spamming users?

565
00:39:21.669 --> 00:39:23.035
Thirty-second answer:

566
00:39:23.315 --> 00:39:35.667
Events become candidates; a ranker decides what to send, coalesces similar events, applies frequency caps and quiet hours, and chooses push versus in-app; outcomes feed back into the model.

567
00:39:37.017 --> 00:39:37.785
Senior.

568
00:39:38.065 --> 00:39:39.110
Question 10.

569
00:39:39.390 --> 00:39:43.657
Explain X3DH and the Double Ratchet at a system-design level.

570
00:39:43.937 --> 00:39:45.302
Thirty-second answer:

571
00:39:45.582 --> 00:40:05.998
X3DH lets Alice establish a shared secret with offline Bob using his published identity, signed prekey and one-time prekey plus her ephemeral key; the Double Ratchet then derives a new key per message (forward secrecy) and mixes fresh Diffie, Hellman values on replies (post-compromise security).

572
00:40:06.278 --> 00:40:13.489
Systems-wise: a key directory, prekey replenishment, per-device sessions, and safety numbers for verification.

573
00:40:14.839 --> 00:40:15.969
Question 11.

574
00:40:16.249 --> 00:40:18.895
How do you guarantee delivery without duplicates?

575
00:40:19.175 --> 00:40:20.540
Thirty-second answer:

576
00:40:20.820 --> 00:40:34.815
Per-device durable queues; messages removed only after device acknowledgement (at-least-once); unique message IDs and client-side dedup give exactly-once display; ordering by sender sequence per conversation.

577
00:40:36.165 --> 00:40:37.253
Question 12.

578
00:40:37.533 --> 00:40:40.263
How do you fight spam when you can't read messages?

579
00:40:40.543 --> 00:40:41.909
Thirty-second answer:

580
00:40:42.189 --> 00:40:58.978
Metadata and behaviour signals (rates, unknown-recipient ratios, blocks, reports), user reports that include recent messages, forwarding limits and labels, registration signals, and graph-based detection of coordinated accounts, with rate limits and bans.

581
00:41:00.328 --> 00:41:01.608
Question 13.

582
00:41:01.888 --> 00:41:04.299
Design the ranking system for the home feed.

583
00:41:04.579 --> 00:41:05.944
Thirty-second answer:

584
00:41:06.224 --> 00:41:26.107
Multiple candidate sources (followed, recommended via embeddings, friends' engagement, ads), filtering, a light early ranker, a heavy multi-task model predicting several actions, a value model with weights tuned for long-term retention, diversity and integrity adjustments, and continuous A or B testing.

585
00:41:27.457 --> 00:41:28.651
Question 14.

586
00:41:28.931 --> 00:41:32.643
How do you generate IDs across thousands of database shards?

587
00:41:32.923 --> 00:41:34.289
Thirty-second answer:

588
00:41:34.569 --> 00:41:48.073
64-bit time-sortable IDs with an embedded logical shard ID and per-shard sequence, generated inside each shard's database; logical shards map onto physical databases so they can be moved.

589
00:41:49.423 --> 00:41:50.319
Architect.

590
00:41:50.599 --> 00:41:51.793
Question 15.

591
00:41:52.073 --> 00:41:56.852
How would you scale a messaging service to hundreds of millions of concurrent connections?

592
00:41:57.132 --> 00:41:58.497
Thirty-second answer:

593
00:41:58.777 --> 00:42:20.751
A large tier of connection servers optimised for many idle sockets (Erlang or epoll), a routing registry for device-to-server mapping, stateless reconnection anywhere, per-device replicated queues, push notifications for offline devices, regional presence with cross-region routing, and graceful rolling deploys that drain sockets gradually.

594
00:42:22.101 --> 00:42:23.295
Question 16.

595
00:42:23.575 --> 00:42:26.135
How would you design content moderation at scale?

596
00:42:26.415 --> 00:42:27.781
Thirty-second answer:

597
00:42:28.061 --> 00:42:47.389
Proactive detection at upload (hash matching, classifiers), risk-based actions (block, restrict, review), prioritised human review with context tools, user reports and appeals, legal takedown workflows per jurisdiction, and measurement (prevalence, precision, time-to-action) with transparency reporting.

598
00:42:48.739 --> 00:42:50.083
Question 17.

599
00:42:50.363 --> 00:42:53.925
What are the trade-offs of keeping chat history only on devices?

600
00:42:54.205 --> 00:42:55.571
Thirty-second answer:

601
00:42:55.851 --> 00:43:12.811
Strong privacy and lower server cost, but harder multi-device history sync, dependence on user backups, loss if the device is lost without backup, and limited server-side features (search, moderation); encrypted backups and device-to-device transfer mitigate.

602
00:43:14.161 --> 00:43:15.334
Question 18.

603
00:43:15.614 --> 00:43:21.715
How do privacy regulations and traceability rules affect an E2EE messenger in India?

604
00:43:21.995 --> 00:43:23.361
Thirty-second answer:

605
00:43:23.641 --> 00:43:46.830
IT Rules 2021 require significant messaging intermediaries to enable identification of a message's first originator on lawful orders, which conflicts with E2EE design and is under legal challenge; the platform must balance compliance, user privacy and security, alongside DPDP Act duties (VERIFY current status).

606
00:43:48.180 --> 00:43:49.417
Question 19.

607
00:43:49.697 --> 00:43:52.940
How do you keep the social graph consistent across regions?

608
00:43:53.220 --> 00:43:54.585
Thirty-second answer:

609
00:43:54.865 --> 00:44:12.252
Leader region per shard handles writes; follower regions serve reads from caches invalidated asynchronously; authors get read-your-writes via routing or version tokens; lease-based cache fills avoid stampedes; eventual consistency is accepted for others' views.

610
00:44:13.602 --> 00:44:14.711
Question 20.

611
00:44:14.991 --> 00:44:18.447
Compare the architectures of a messenger and a social feed.

612
00:44:18.727 --> 00:44:20.093
Thirty-second answer:

613
00:44:20.373 --> 00:44:38.805
The messenger is a privacy-preserving relay with device-held state, per-device queues and E2EE, optimising delivery and connections; the feed is a server-side content system with a social graph, media processing, ranking and moderation, optimising relevance and read throughput.

614
00:44:39.085 --> 00:44:43.757
They share identity, media infrastructure, notifications and abuse systems.

615
00:44:45.107 --> 00:44:46.152
Part 13.

616
00:44:46.432 --> 00:44:47.200
Summary.

617
00:44:48.450 --> 00:44:59.202
The messenger is a relay with cryptography at the edges: keys and history live on devices; the server moves ciphertext through per-device queues and connection servers.

618
00:44:59.482 --> 00:45:09.829
Signal-protocol building blocks (X3DH, Double Ratchet, sender keys; MLS for large groups) give forward secrecy and post-compromise security.

619
00:45:10.109 --> 00:45:12.221
Safety numbers let users verify.

620
00:45:12.501 --> 00:45:22.997
The social feed is a graph plus a ranking funnel: objects and associations with massive caches, hybrid fan-out, multi-stage ranking tuned for long-term value.

621
00:45:24.347 --> 00:45:30.491
Media dominates storage and egress: process once, hash for integrity, serve from CDNs.

622
00:45:30.771 --> 00:45:42.888
Integrity is built in: metadata-based abuse control for E2EE, proactive detection and human review for public content, and legal and regulatory processes per jurisdiction.

